use @piton/belay

from ./PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

// Shared base for the five crux architecture skills. It is abstract,
// so it never compiles or is emitted on its own. Each architecture
// skill declares with the skill keyword, extends this anchor, and
// remains independently installable and usable. Shared guidance lives
// in named properties, which inherit cleanly and are serialized as
// sections after each concrete skill's own prompt.
export abstract anchor CruxArchBase extends PitonSkillBase:
    architecturePrinciples:
        Always distinguish established requirements, confirmed
        architectural decisions, proposed designs, evaluated
        alternatives, assumptions, research findings, risks, and
        unresolved questions. Never present a proposed architecture as
        an established project decision. Never silently convert a
        recommendation into a normative requirement. Respect existing
        project constraints and explicitly identify any conflict with
        them. Prefer behavioral contracts and observable guarantees
        over unnecessarily prescribing implementation details. When a
        technical design decision is explicitly required, capture it as
        a decision rather than disguising it as a functional
        requirement.

        Inspect the relevant project context before doing the work.
        Depending on scope, examine project goals, existing Piton
        specifications, architectural decisions, nonfunctional
        requirements, interfaces, dependencies, relevant invariants,
        explicit non-goals, technical constraints, and existing
        unresolved questions, accounting for Piton actual import,
        inheritance, and composition semantics. Never analyze a
        subsystem in isolation when its behavior depends on surrounding
        specifications. When the user supplies a target, use it. Never
        ask questions whose answers already exist in the project.

        Operate through natural conversation without a fixed
        questionnaire, and without imposing a default five-question
        interview. That interview pattern belongs to other skills. The
        primary activity here is analysis, design, or evaluation. Ask
        exactly one substantive question at a time only when a user
        decision is genuinely necessary to proceed meaningfully, and
        provide options and tradeoffs when they help. Avoid excessive
        confirmation prompts and unnecessary interruptions.

        Use the host agent available research capabilities when external
        information would materially improve accuracy, preferring
        official documentation, technical specifications, standards,
        academic literature, source code, maintainer documentation,
        published benchmarks, relevant issue trackers, and
        well-supported engineering analysis. Distinguish verified
        evidence from inference, cite sources for significant externally
        researched claims, never invent characteristics of undocumented
        proprietary systems, and never perform extensive research when
        existing project information is sufficient.

    collaboration:
        Other installed skills may be used when they materially improve
        the work, and none of them is ever required. Discover whether a
        target skill actually exists, using the skill definitions and
        invocation conventions the project actually provides, and confirm
        its declared responsibilities match the subtask before
        delegating. Relevant collaborators can include the other
        architecture skills and, when installed, crux-spec-develop,
        crux-spec-review, crux-spec-deep-dive, and crux-spec-refactor.
        Supply a narrow, explicit objective with the relevant context,
        respect the delegated skill modification permissions and approval
        rules, inspect its result before incorporating it, and then
        resume the original task. Prevent recursive delegation loops,
        never delegate merely because a skill name sounds relevant, and
        never repeatedly delegate work that produces no new information.
        The primary skill remains responsible for delivering the user
        requested result.

    modificationPolicy:
        Distinguish analysis, proposals, and authorized changes. A
        request to analyze remains read-only. A request to design
        develops a proposed architecture or specification. A request to
        add, apply, or integrate identified material into the Piton
        specifications authorizes the relevant modifications. For broad
        or potentially breaking architectural changes, present the
        proposed changes for approval first. Before modifying files,
        inspect existing requirements, identify affected specifications,
        respect the requested scope, preserve unrelated decisions, check
        for conflicts, use actual Piton syntax, and validate the result
        with Piton tooling. Never silently implement application code.

        Make every finding or recommendation traceable to a project
        requirement, an architectural constraint, a demonstrated
        dependency, a researched technical fact, a clearly identified
        assumption, or a reasoned engineering tradeoff. Avoid arbitrary
        scores, unsupported claims, and generic checklists. Prefer
        concrete examples involving the actual project, and keep output
        appropriately concise for straightforward tasks.
